ich were fast but limited by weather, daylight, and line of sight. By the 1830s, several inventors were working on sending messages along wires using electricity. In Britain, William Fothergill Cooke and Charles Wheatstone developed a needle telegraph that deflected magnetized needles to point at letters. In the United States, Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed a system that sent pulses of current representing dots and dashes, which operators translated into text.\n\nMorse's first public demonstration came in 1844, when he sent the message \"What hath God wrought\" over a line between Washington, D.C., and Baltimore. Within a few years, telegraph lines spread across the eastern United States and western Europe, and they carried commercial prices, news, and government orders at speeds that had previously taken days.\n\nThe telegraph also changed how news moved. Newspapers used telegraph reports, and the wire services that formed to share them shaped how stories were written, with the most important facts placed first in case a line failed mid-message. The transatlantic cable, completed in 1858 and then working intermittently, showed both the promise and the difficulty of the technology. Later, more reliable cables carried messages across oceans and empires.\n\nTelegraphy also created new professions, notably operators, who became known for speed and skill, and it spurred standardization. Morse code's international form and the later Telegraph Union helped establish common rules for signaling across borders.\n\nThe telegraph was eventually displaced by the telephone, then by radio and digital networks, but it established the core idea of near-instant communication over distance. Its legacy shows up in everyday terms such as \"wire\" for news dispatches and in the expectation that information should travel faster than the people carrying it.","ttft_ms":1440,"type":"result","duration_ms":8464,"uuid":"e7513e87-293a-4ec1-94d3-8be3094192a6","ttft_stream_ms":1207,"time_